## Summary — Bento4/mp42aac PoC Results

| # | Vulnerability | Status | ASAN Finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| 001 | AP4_CttsAtom — entry_count×8 integer overflow | **VERIFIED_CRASH** | `heap-buffer-overflow` READ in `AP4_CttsAtom::AP4_CttsAtom` |
| 002 | AP4_Stz2Atom — table_size overflow bypasses bounds check | **VERIFIED_CRASH** | `heap-buffer-overflow` READ in `AP4_Stz2Atom::AP4_Stz2Atom` |
| 003 | AP4_TrunAtom — unchecked SetItemCount → OOM crash | **VERIFIED_CRASH** | `rss-limit-exceeded` in `operator new` during trun parsing |

All PoC files are under `/data/ylwang/non-textfuzz/target/_poc/Bento4/Source_C++_Core_Ap4Config_h/`. Each was triggered purely via a crafted MP4 passed to the pre-built ASAN+UBSAN `mp42aac` binary — no C/C++ compilation involved.

**Key observations:**
- VULNs 001 and 002 both land as heap-buffer-overflow on the very first loop iteration after a 32-bit integer overflow produces a near-zero allocation. ASAN catches these immediately.
- VULN 003 on a 64-bit build with memory overcommit manifests as rss-limit-exceeded rather than a null dereference (the 4 GB allocation succeeds virtually but exhausts physical memory), which is still a reliable DoS crash path.
